\vspace{0.2em}
\begin{abstract}
% AMBIGUITY_ABSTRACT_START
Does departure from gender stereotypes carry an electoral penalty, and does that penalty depend on political context? We exploit an observational natural experiment to examine electoral inequality within gender categories. This classification rests on the assumption that naturally arising facial variation introduces a probabilistic component into gendered appearance beyond candidates' precise control. It does not establish exchangeability or causal identification. We built an original dataset linking separate femininity, masculinity, and attractiveness ratings to official personal-vote counts in Finland's \Sexpr{paper_values$election_year} open-list municipal elections. Exploiting complete records for \Sexpr{n_main} candidates, we estimate Poisson conditional-mean models with municipality and party fixed effects, adjusting for age and attractiveness. A gender-relative cue contrast increases with femininity relative to masculinity among men and the reverse among women. A one-standard-deviation increase is associated with differences in expected votes of \Sexpr{effect_text("Male")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text("Female")} for women. \Sexpr{primary_abstract_short_text} \Sexpr{party_abstract_context_text} Many individual party contrasts remain imprecise. These findings connect infracategorical inequality to the distribution of personal support across gendered appearance profiles and political contexts, without identifying the causal processes producing those differences.
% AMBIGUITY_ABSTRACT_END
\end{abstract}
\vspace{0.4em}
\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.9\textwidth}
\small\noindent\textbf{Keywords} electoral inequality, gender typicality, natural experiment, candidate appearance, personal votes, Finland.
\end{minipage}
\end{center}
\end{titlepage}
\pagenumbering{arabic}
\hypersetup{pageanchor=true}

% AMBIGUITY_MAIN_START
\section{The problem of inequality within gender categories}
\label{sec:introduction}

If attractive candidates tend to receive more votes \parencite{BerggrenEtAl2010}, can a \emph{departure} from gender stereotypes nevertheless carry an electoral \emph{penalty}? Consider a woman with a relatively masculine face or a man with a relatively feminine face: both may be attractive, yet their appearances depart from the cues expected of their gender. This departure raises a question about who benefits from appearance, beyond who is considered ``good-looking.'' The distinction matters because appearance advantages are already distributed unequally by gender. In Finnish municipal elections, \textcite{Bahamonde2024} find that attractiveness is more strongly associated with votes among men. Related electoral research likewise finds that appearance-based trait inferences have different implications for male and female candidates \parencite{DitontoMattes2018}. These asymmetries motivate asking whether conformity to gender stereotypes is itself a source of unequal support among candidates within each gender category.

Moreover, does a penalty for gender-atypical appearance persist across ideological audiences, or can political context turn the same departure into an advantage? There is reason to expect the answer to vary: beauty premiums differ across political camps \parencite{BerggrenEtAl2017}, and the electoral associations of facial sex typicality differ by candidate gender and party \parencite{Carpinella2016}. Audience ideology also changes the value of facial dominance \parencite{LaustsenPetersen2016}. These findings suggest that a cue's electoral meaning depends on who evaluates it and what they expect from political leaders. A uniform conformity penalty may therefore miss contexts in which a gender-atypical cue conveys a desirable leadership trait.

This possibility shifts the inequality question from differences between men and women to distinctions \emph{within} each category. A femininity--masculinity balance that confers an advantage on one man can distinguish him from other men, just as a different balance can distinguish one woman from other women. Such distinctions fit \textcite{Monk2022}'s infracategorical perspective. Shared category membership need not imply shared opportunities or evaluations. Applied to elections, this perspective locates inequality in \emph{the unequal allocation of personal support associated with different appearance profiles among candidates who share a gender category}. The issue is therefore not simply whether men outperform women, but which men and which women receive more support and how political context conditions those differences.

Existing research makes this within-category question tractable. \textcite{Hehman2014} relate competition between perceived gender categories to female politicians' electoral outcomes, while studies of gender expression document associations with election returns \parencite{Wojcik2024} and experimentally induced presentation penalties for male candidates \parencite{Naunov2026}. \textcite{Park2023} further show how separately rated masculinity and femininity in campaign photographs relate to vote share. Building on these approaches, we construct a continuous within-gender contrast of greater femininity relative to masculinity among men and greater masculinity relative to femininity among women. This measure allows a comparison of departure from gender expectations while preserving the distinction between atypicality and the value of either cue alone.

To connect that comparison to electoral inequality, we built an original dataset combining separate appearance ratings with candidate characteristics and official personal-vote counts in Finland's \Sexpr{paper_values$election_year} municipal elections. We exploit this dataset in a setting where voters select individual candidates within party lists. Because personal votes determine candidates' rank within those lists, while list totals determine the allocation of seats, unequal personal support can matter for access to representation \parencite{Bahamonde2024,HolliWass2010,SoderlundVonSchoultz2024}. The separate ratings and linked returns allow us to examine inequality within gender and then within gender--party groups, conditional on other candidate attributes and electoral context.

We exploit naturally arising variation in gendered appearance as an \emph{observational natural experiment} \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. The classification rests on an explicit assignment assumption that inherited and developmental variation introduces an element of chance into candidates' gender-atypical cue profiles beyond their precise control. Finnish municipal elections provide a substantive setting for this assumption because parties recruit ordinary residents to extensive candidate lists and campaign communication prominently uses headshots \parencite[628--629]{Bahamonde2024}. These practices make it plausible that naturally arising differences remain visible in electoral competition. The external probabilistic component distinguishes this design as a subtype of observational study \parencite{Titiunik2021}, while its assignment probabilities remain unknown. Causal identification would additionally require comparability and a well-defined intervention. We therefore investigate conditional inequalities in personal support rather than treating natural assignment as evidence of exchangeability.\footnote{\autoref{sec:oa-natural-experiment} of the Online Appendix formalizes the assignment argument for the continuous appearance contrast, distinguishes assignment from measurement, and sets out the additional assumptions that causal inference would require.}

Our argument is that the electoral consequences of departing from gendered appearance expectations depend on what voters want from a candidate. Conformity to those expectations could bring more votes if voters prefer men who look conventionally masculine and women who look conventionally feminine. A man who appears more feminine, or a woman who appears more masculine, could then receive less support than other candidates of the same gender. Yet the same departure could bring more votes if it suggests qualities that voters value in political leadership. For example, a relatively feminine male appearance might suggest warmth or cooperation. Because voters may value these qualities differently across political settings, we expect the relationship between gendered appearance and electoral support to vary across parties.

We assess this argument by exploiting the observational natural experiment through an original dataset that links separate appearance ratings to personal votes for \Sexpr{n_main} candidates in \Sexpr{n_cities} municipalities. We estimate Poisson count models with municipality and party fixed effects and adjust for candidate age and attractiveness. We first compare candidates within gender categories and then allow the appearance association to differ by party. A one-standard-deviation increase in the gender-atypical index is associated with differences in expected personal votes of \Sexpr{effect_text("Male")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text("Female")} for women. \Sexpr{primary_abstract_text} \Sexpr{party_abstract_context_text} These findings locate electoral inequality among candidates who share a gender category. Because personal votes help determine candidates' positions on electoral lists, differences in that support can matter for access to representation. They also show why gender issues in electoral appearance cannot be understood solely through a comparison of men as a group with women as a group.

Our contribution is to examine whether the relative balance of separately perceived femininity and masculinity is associated with electoral disadvantage or advantage \emph{within gender categories and party contexts}. Existing studies establish gender-by-party differences, but their measurement choices constrain the comparisons they can make. The human ratings in \textcite[26]{Carpinella2016} use a scale from ``1 = very masculine to 9 = very feminine'', placing the two cues at opposite ends of one dimension. \textcite[6,11--12]{Park2023} rate femininity and masculinity separately but report that ``we exclude the traits with low agreement'', which excludes attractiveness from the traits available for their vote-share regressions. We exploit an original dataset of separately elicited, gender-referenced human ratings from a population-based survey and actual personal-vote counts in Finnish open-list elections to examine relative cue balance while accounting for measured attractiveness, age, party, and municipality. Methodologically, we develop an observational natural-experiment argument that makes the external assignment premise and its limits explicit. This assignment argument connects naturally arising appearance variation to electoral inequality without claiming that the design identifies causal effects. The baseline model reveals an appearance-associated inequality that is unexpected under the category-conformity account, with more gender-atypical male profiles receiving greater expected personal votes. The female association remains uncertain, and the estimates do not establish a gender difference. Party estimates suggest advantages in some settings and disadvantages in others, although many contrasts remain imprecise. Together, these findings locate electoral inequality within gender categories and party settings.

This paper proceeds as follows. In \autoref{sec:theory}, we develop the competing expectations about conformity to gendered appearance and the qualities voters seek in leaders. In \autoref{sec:data}, we describe the original dataset, the rating procedure, and the gender-atypical index. In \autoref{sec:strategy}, we develop the observational natural-experiment classification, explain the Poisson specification, and distinguish statistical from causal identification. In \autoref{sec:results}, we present the main findings, examine differences across parties, and assess whether measured attractiveness accounts for the observed appearance-associated inequality. In \autoref{sec:discussion}, we consider the implications for gender and electoral representation, the limitations of the study, and directions for future research.

\section{Gender typicality and electoral evaluation}
\label{sec:theory}

Candidates who share a gender category can differ in how masculine and feminine they appear. \textcite{Monk2022} explains why a shared category does not imply that its members are perceived in the same way. Some members look more typical of that category than others, and these differences can be associated with unequal evaluations. For candidates, the relevant comparison is therefore among men or among women whose appearances differ. Research on gender variation also supports considering femininity and masculinity separately rather than reducing them to one male--female scale \parencite{Magliozzi2016}. We use separate ratings to describe their relative balance within each gender category. This allows us to ask why some balances might be associated with more electoral support than others.

Voters may prefer candidates who conform to gendered appearance expectations by looking conventionally masculine as men or conventionally feminine as women. The \emph{category-conformity account} predicts that men with more feminine-relative-to-masculine appearances and women with more masculine-relative-to-feminine appearances will receive fewer votes than more gender-typical candidates of the same gender. Rewarding conformity would therefore distribute support unequally among men and among women.

Such a preference could arise if appearances that match gender expectations make candidates easier to classify as male or female. \textcite{Owen2016} show that faces combining gender cues can become less appealing when observers are required to categorize them. Categorization difficulty need not, however, produce disapproval. \textcite{Ansani2024} find positive judgments of androgynous faces despite uncertainty about their category. For difficulty classifying a candidate's apparent gender to contribute to fewer votes, it would also have to lead voters to evaluate that candidate less favorably. Our ratings do not measure categorization difficulty, so it remains a possible explanation rather than an observed process.

Voters may instead value an appearance that suggests desirable leadership qualities even when it departs from expectations about men and women. The \emph{leadership fit account} consequently concerns the match between the qualities attributed to a candidate and those sought in a leader \parencite{Eagly2002}. Experiments by \textcite[1]{Walker2017} illustrate this possibility. After manipulating the faces of clearly male and female targets, they report that ``Masculine-looking persons were seen as colder and more competent than feminine-looking persons.'' An appearance can therefore suggest both a favorable and an unfavorable trait without making the person's gender unclear. Evidence from politicians' portraits shows that these judgments can also differ across political contexts. \textcite[775]{CarpinellaJohnson2013b} found that less sex-typical, more feminine male liberal politicians were judged ``as significantly warmer than their more sex-typical counterparts''. The corresponding warmth association was not established for conservative men.

Whether these impressions bring more votes depends on which qualities voters want. \textcite[5]{SpisakWarriors2012} examined this question in mock elections that varied whether leaders faced war or peacekeeping demands. They summarize their findings as follows. ``In sum, irrespective of their sex, masculine-looking leaders were preferred during war and feminine-looking leaders during peacekeeping.'' Their experiment varied facial cues within male and female targets, showing that the preferred appearance could change with the task. If voters seek cooperation, a relatively feminine male appearance could seem better suited to leadership even though it departs from masculine appearance expectations. Such a preference could favor some male appearance profiles over others. This explanation of inequality among men depends on the qualities voters seek rather than on conformity alone.

The audience provides another reason for the same appearance to be evaluated differently. \textcite[abstract]{LaustsenPetersen2016} find that ``a non-dominant face constitutes a winning face among liberal audiences'', while the advantage reverses among conservatives. Their study concerns dominance, which is different from our femininity--masculinity contrast. It nevertheless shows that an appearance cue can have different electoral value depending on who evaluates it. Evidence from exit-poll respondents also indicates that appearance associations differ across audiences. Republican voters favored candidates with stereotypically conservative-looking faces, whereas Democratic voters showed no corresponding association \parencite{Olivola2018}. These findings provide a reason to examine political context when studying unequal support within gender categories.

Together, this research suggests a possible explanation for an advantage associated with a gender-atypical male appearance. Relatively feminine cues might suggest warmth or cooperation that some voters value in a leader. That possibility concerns perceived appearance and should not be assumed to apply to every form of gender presentation.\footnote{In experiments on campaign language, \textcite[1195]{Wang2023} found that presenting male candidates in communal terms emphasizing relationships and others' needs reduced overall warmth and likability without a significant vote-choice effect. Different forms of gender presentation therefore cannot be assumed to produce the same evaluations.} Its application to Finnish municipal elections remains a hypothesis. The institutional setting alone does not establish that voters prioritized cooperation, and our electoral associations do not show which qualities voters inferred from candidates' photographs.

\subsection{Theoretical expectations}

The preceding accounts imply three expectations about inequality within gender categories. The \emph{conformity expectation} predicts that a more gender-atypical appearance will be associated with fewer votes among both men and women. Men whose appearance is more feminine relative to masculine should receive less support than more conventionally masculine men. Women whose appearance is more masculine relative to feminine should receive less support than more conventionally feminine women. In each comparison, conformity would distinguish candidates who receive more support from others of the same gender.

Under the \emph{cooperative-leadership expectation}, an appearance suggesting warmth or cooperation will be favored where voters seek those qualities in a leader. If voters favor a more feminine-relative-to-masculine balance in both genders, that balance should be associated with more votes among men and among women. It would represent a more gender-atypical appearance among men but a more gender-typical appearance among women. The same preference could therefore reward atypicality among men and conformity among women. Where voters instead seek qualities associated with masculine appearance in competitive leadership, the expected pattern reverses \parencite{SpisakWarriors2012}. These expectations remain conditional because municipal office alone does not establish which leadership qualities voters wanted.

The \emph{contextual expectation} predicts that the relationship between appearance and votes will differ across parties if voters in those settings value different leadership qualities. Earlier findings of candidate gender-by-party differences in facial typicality and campaign imagery support examining this possibility \parencite{Carpinella2016,Park2023}. Party comparisons therefore ask whether an overall association among men or among women conceals different patterns of support within those categories. This expectation does not require a single left--right ordering of appearance advantages. A candidate's party, a voter's ideology, and the qualities inferred from appearance are different attributes. Electoral associations matching an expectation would be consistent with the relevant account, but they would not establish that account's causal explanation. Evidence on voter perceptions and evaluations would be needed to determine how appearance contributes to unequal support.

\section{Data and measurement}
\label{sec:data}
The argument requires measures of how citizens perceive candidates and electoral support for those same candidates. We therefore built an original dataset by linking official candidate records and personal-vote counts from Finland's \Sexpr{paper_values$election_year} municipal elections to evaluations of campaign photographs. The Ministry of Justice's returns provide the electoral information.\footnote{Ministry of Justice, \Sexpr{paper_values$election_year} municipal-election results are available in the \href{https://tulospalvelu.vaalit.fi/KV-2017/fi/ladattavat_tiedostot.html}{candidate-level electoral data}.} The photographs are the official campaign portraits that candidates used on election posters and in newspaper advertisements alongside their names and party affiliations \parencite{Bahamonde2024,BerggrenEtAl2010,BerggrenEtAl2017}. Using these images connects the appearance measurements to the visual information available during an actual campaign.

The collection began with more than \Sexpr{format(paper_values$photo_pool_min,big.mark=",",scientific=FALSE,trim=TRUE)} photographs from a universe of \Sexpr{format(paper_values$candidate_universe,big.mark=",",scientific=FALSE,trim=TRUE)} candidates. We randomly selected \Sexpr{format(paper_values$photos_selected,big.mark=",",scientific=FALSE,trim=TRUE)} photographs with the aim of covering candidate gender and backgrounds across Finland \parencite{Bahamonde2024}. Image editing removed identifying information, and a pilot excluded photographs of insufficient quality. These steps prepared a set of campaign portraits for evaluation without relying on names or party labels to convey the appearance attributes.

To obtain the evaluations, we fielded a population-based survey in \Sexpr{paper_values$rating_year}. Invitations and a reminder were sent to a random sample of \Sexpr{format(paper_values$n_invited,big.mark=",",scientific=FALSE,trim=TRUE)} Finnish-speaking adults aged \Sexpr{paper_values$respondent_age_min}--\Sexpr{paper_values$respondent_age_max} from the population register. A total of \Sexpr{format(paper_values$n_respondents,big.mark=",",scientific=FALSE,trim=TRUE)} people participated. We exploit attractiveness, masculinity, and femininity ratings supplied by separate attribute-specific respondent groups. Each respondent evaluated \Sexpr{paper_values$images_per_respondent} randomly assigned photographs for the assigned attribute \parencite{Bahamonde2024}. Separate groups therefore supplied the three sets of judgments, while random photograph assignment distributed the images across raters. These randomization procedures concern sampling and measurement. The natural-experiment classification instead concerns the assumed probabilistic component in the formation of candidates' appearance before electoral competition.

Direct citizen ratings capture the perceptions central to our argument by assessing how masculine or feminine a candidate appears relative to others of the same age and gender. A software-based classification of facial structure or inferred sex would require further validation before it could be interpreted as the same gender-referenced judgment. Recruiting raters from a population sample also widens the range of citizens contributing evaluations compared with the convenience samples common in earlier electoral appearance research \parencite{Mattes2010,Rosar2008,WiggintonStockemer2021,Bahamonde2024}. The method thus brings the appearance measure closer to the evaluations relevant to our argument.

The survey also took steps to reduce the influence of candidate recognition and uneven exposure. Respondents did not evaluate candidates from their own electoral district. Images recognized by a majority of their raters were excluded, amounting to \Sexpr{paper_values$recognition_exclusions} photographs. The assignment procedure gave priority to images that had received fewer ratings, helping distribute evaluations across the photograph pool. A neutral screen with a university logo appeared between images, and respondents could take as much time as needed to provide their judgments \parencite{Bahamonde2024}. These procedures supported evaluations of appearance while limiting cues from familiarity with the candidate.

The femininity and masculinity questions asked how the person looked relative to \emph{others of the same age and gender}, using separate \Sexpr{paper_values$scale_min}--\Sexpr{paper_values$scale_max} scales \parencite{Bahamonde2024,HossEtAl2005}. Respondents were reminded that men and women can both appear masculine or feminine. These reference categories are central to our question: a high masculinity rating for a woman locates her relative to other women, while a high femininity rating for a man locates him relative to other men. The questions therefore describe variation within gender categories without treating the appearance ratings as measures of gender identity.

Attractiveness was rated relative to others of the same age and gender as a separate attribute, following the measurement precedents used in \textcite{Bono2017,GriffinLanglois2006,Tu2022}. This separation is important because a gendered appearance might also be judged attractive. It allows us to exploit the original dataset to examine whether the appearance-associated difference in votes remains after accounting for measured attractiveness. \autoref{sec:attractiveness} develops that comparison.

We average each attribute's ratings for each candidate, following electoral appearance research that aggregates multiple observers' judgments \parencite{BerggrenEtAl2010}. Matching the available candidate averages with electoral records gives an original dataset of \Sexpr{nrow(dat_source)} candidates with separate appearance measurements and personal-vote counts. We exploit complete observations on the baseline model's variables for \Sexpr{n_male} men and \Sexpr{n_female} women in \Sexpr{n_cities} municipalities. Linking these judgments to support for the same candidates makes it possible to examine electoral inequality within gender and political context.

We construct the \emph{gender-atypical cue contrast} in \autoref{eq:axes} from femininity and masculinity ratings standardized separately within each gender category. Centering each trait on its gender-group mean and scaling it by its gender-group standard deviation yields candidate $i$'s scores $z^F_i$ and $z^M_i$. The direction factor $s_g=+1$ for men and $s_g=-1$ for women orients the contrast toward femininity relative to masculinity among men and masculinity relative to femininity among women. The resulting index is
\begin{equation}
 A_i=\frac{s_g(z^F_i-z^M_i)}{\operatorname{sd}_g(z^F-z^M)}.
 \label{eq:axes}
\end{equation}
Higher $A$ therefore captures a more feminine-relative-to-masculine appearance among men and a more masculine-relative-to-feminine appearance among women. Scaling the contrast by the within-gender standard deviation of $z^F-z^M$ expresses these departures in a common unit, with one unit of $A$ corresponding to one standard deviation within either gender category.

Figure~\ref{fig:cue-support} displays the separate ratings underlying the index in a single panel. Each point places a candidate according to perceived femininity on the horizontal axis and perceived masculinity on the vertical axis. Blue points represent men and pink points represent women, allowing the two distributions and the variation within each gender category to be inspected together. The overlay preserves the same-age and same-gender reference of the survey questions, so the index continues to locate each candidate within their gender category.

For illustration, a man whose masculinity rating is average among men and whose femininity rating is one standard deviation above the male average has $A=\Sexpr{fmt(paper_values$cue_example_A["Male"],2)}$ in \autoref{eq:axes}. A woman whose femininity rating is average among women and whose masculinity rating is one standard deviation above the female average has $A=\Sexpr{fmt(paper_values$cue_example_A["Female"],2)}$. In the combined panel of Figure~\ref{fig:cue-support}, the crosses locate each gender group's mean profile, and the arrows lead from those means to the hypothetical profiles marked by hollow diamonds. The male example shifts rightward at fixed masculinity, while the female example shifts upward at fixed femininity. These hypothetical profiles both yield positive index values, capturing the relatively feminine male appearance and the relatively masculine female appearance as departures from their respective gender groups' appearance expectations.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/cue_support.pdf}
\caption{Perceived femininity and masculinity by candidate gender in a combined panel. Blue points show mean ratings for \Sexpr{n_male} men and pink points show mean ratings for \Sexpr{n_female} women. Ratings were elicited relative to others of the same age and gender. Crosses mark gender-group average ratings, and arrows lead to the two hypothetical profiles marked by hollow diamonds. The dashed diagonal marks equal raw ratings on the \Sexpr{paper_values$scale_min}--\Sexpr{paper_values$scale_max} scales. The gender-atypical contrast is standardized within gender using \autoref{eq:axes}.}
\label{fig:cue-support}
\end{figure}

Because the ratings are gender-referenced, $A=0$ denotes an average relative contrast within the candidate's gender category. This reference point is determined by the gender-specific centering and scaling in \autoref{eq:axes}, whereas the diagonal in Figure~\ref{fig:cue-support} compares the two raw ratings. Zero on the index does not mark a boundary at which observers become uncertain about gender. The index thus measures one aspect of perceived appearance rather than gender identity or categorization difficulty.\footnote{We also estimate balance between the raw ratings, controlling for their mean intensity. \autoref{sec:oa-balance} of the Online Appendix provides this alternative measure's definition and estimates.}

Because the contrast locates candidates within their gender category, Table~\ref{tab:summary} reports the outcome, appearance measures, and covariates separately for men and women in the sample that we exploit below. This organization shows the variation within each category on which the subsequent comparison of electoral inequality rests.

