\section{Empirical strategy}
\label{sec:strategy}
\subsection{An observational natural experiment}
\label{sec:natural-experiment}
We treat naturally arising gendered appearance as an observational natural experiment, conditional on the assignment premise set out below \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. Researchers neither designed nor implemented the processes that generated candidates' appearance, and the full probabilities of alternative appearance allocations cannot be recovered. The premise is that these processes include a probabilistic component outside candidates' precise control. This argument concerns the origin of appearance variation. It does not imply equal assignment probabilities or independence from potential electoral outcomes because external chance can coexist with systematic differences in candidates' electoral prospects.

For our application, candidates are the units and the continuous gender-atypical cue contrast describes their appearance condition. The external factor is naturally arising variation in inherited facial morphology and development. Genetic contributions to normal facial morphology are documented by \textcite{LiuEtAl2012}. We make the further assignment assumption that an uncontrollable component of this variation affects the balance of gendered cues visible in campaign portraits, leaving candidates with nondegenerate ex ante possibilities for the gender-atypical contrast itself. The biological evidence motivates this premise but does not verify the assignment law for our index. Candidates can influence grooming, expression, and portrait selection while remaining unable to choose every feature of their face precisely. An external chance component can therefore persist alongside systematic presentation choices \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. The counterfactual allocation probabilities cannot be reconstructed from the portraits, ratings, and returns available in the study. Our natural-experiment claim accordingly rests on a substantive assignment assumption, rather than a recovered or implemented randomization protocol.

The Finnish municipal setting supports the plausibility that naturally arising differences remain visible among candidates. Parties have incentives to assemble extensive lists and actively recruit ordinary residents, including individuals who are not pursuing professional political careers \parencite[628]{Bahamonde2024}. The emphasis on functionality and frugality is also reflected in portraits showing everyday clothing alongside formal attire. Campaign communication prominently relies on headshots in posters and newspaper advertisements, with limited individual television and radio campaigning \parencite[628--629]{Bahamonde2024}\parencite[80,83]{BerggrenEtAl2017}. These features provide contextual support for the assignment premise by linking a broad candidate pool to routine representations of appearance. They do not establish random entry into politics, equal candidate exposure, or the absence of strategic presentation. The assignment assumption must therefore apply to the candidates who enter our sample, independently of the contextual evidence supporting it.

We exploit this natural experiment to characterize conditional electoral inequality. Probabilistic appearance assignment does not establish unconfoundedness or equiprobable assignment, and the study has no known randomization distribution for inference \parencite[119,122--126]{Titiunik2021}. We consequently do not assume that candidates with different cue profiles are causally exchangeable. Municipality and party fixed effects and candidate controls define the statistical comparison, while uncertainty is estimated from the observed vote model. \autoref{sec:oa-natural-experiment} formalizes the extension from binary treatment assignment to a continuous appearance condition, the distinction between latent appearance and its ratings, and the additional restrictions that causal identification would require. The main estimates remain conditional associations in personal support.

\subsection{Conditional vote-count specification}
\label{sec:vote-model}
The outcome \texttt{Votes}$_i$ is the number of personal votes received by candidate $i$. Finnish municipal elections use open-list proportional representation. Each voter selects an individual candidate, and the vote also contributes to the candidate's electoral list. Pooled list votes determine the allocation of seats, while personal votes determine candidates' positions within the list \parencite[604,626]{HolliWass2010}. These rules make personal support important for competition among candidates on the same list and for access to representation \parencite[13,16]{SoderlundVonSchoultz2024}. Votes therefore provide a substantive measure of how electoral support is distributed among candidates. This choice also connects our measurement of electoral support to appearance research using actual election returns \parencite{Efran1974,Bahamonde2024}.

We exploit the original dataset to estimate the expected number of votes conditional on candidate appearance and electoral context. Following \textcite{Bahamonde2024}, we use Poisson pseudo-maximum likelihood (PPML) to estimate the conditional expected vote count. The Poisson specification relates that expectation to the gender-atypical index, while municipality fixed effects account for differences in the scale and shared context of local elections. Party fixed effects account for differences in support across parties. Age and attractiveness enter as candidate controls, and an interaction allows the association between atypicality and votes to differ between men and women.

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mu_i &= \mathrm{E}[\texttt{Votes}_i\mid
 \texttt{Atypicality}_i,\texttt{Woman}_i,\mathbf{X}_i,c(i),p(i)],
 \\[-0.1em]
 \log\mu_i &= \alpha_{c(i)}+\lambda_{p(i)}+\beta_1\texttt{Woman}_i
 +\beta_2\texttt{Atypicality}_i
 +\beta_3(\texttt{Woman}_i\times\texttt{Atypicality}_i)
 \\[-0.1em]
 &\quad+\beta_4\texttt{Age}_i+\beta_5\texttt{Attractiveness}_i.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:model}
\end{equation}
With \texttt{Atypicality} given by $A$ in \autoref{eq:axes} and \texttt{Woman} indicating a female candidate, the index's association with log expected votes is $\beta_2$ among men and $\beta_2+\beta_3$ among women. The log link yields positive expected vote counts and expresses the appearance association proportionally, allowing percentage differences in support to be compared across municipalities of different sizes. Estimation uses the observed vote counts in their original units.

Statistical identification of the model coefficients relies on variation in appearance and votes after accounting for municipality and party differences, mean-centered age, and measured attractiveness. The fixed effects remove common differences across municipalities and parties, but they do not remove every candidate-specific influence on support. In particular, recognition, campaign resources, and selection into candidacy can remain related to both appearance and votes. The model therefore estimates appearance-associated differences in expected support conditional on the observed controls. Causal identification would additionally require a well-defined appearance intervention and restrictions ensuring exchangeability, adequate support, and consistency. The specification does not establish those restrictions or identify the effect of changing a candidate's appearance.\footnote{We also adjust for joint cue level, separating the combined rating levels from their contrast in Online Appendix Figures~\ref{fig:oa-joint-votes} and~\ref{fig:oa-joint-share}. Quadratic terms allow the appearance association to change across index values and depend on how the cues combine. Table~\ref{tab:oa-quadratic} reports the coefficients, Table~\ref{tab:oa-nonlinear-tests} their joint tests, and Figure~\ref{fig:oa-surface} the fitted surface.}

The Poisson specification targets this conditional mean without requiring the outcome's entire distribution to be Poisson when the mean is correctly specified \parencite{SantosSilva2006}. The conditional variance may therefore differ from the mean. We also allow outcomes to be dependent among candidates in the same municipality, using municipality-clustered sandwich standard errors with a finite-sample correction and $t_{G-1}$ intervals, where $G$ is the number of municipalities. These intervals condition on the candidate mean ratings used to construct the contrast and do not propagate uncertainty in those estimated means. \autoref{sec:oa-diagnostics} of the Online Appendix reports dispersion and sample diagnostics.

We use the fitted model to express the appearance association as expected personal votes. At each value of the gender-atypical index, we predict votes for every candidate while retaining the candidate's observed age, attractiveness, party, and municipality. We then average these predictions separately among men and among women.\footnote{Predictions for the $n_g$ candidates in gender group $g$ are averaged at index value $a$, retaining each candidate's other observed characteristics. The resulting average is \begin{equation}\overline{\mu}_g(a)=\frac{1}{n_g}\sum_{i:g(i)=g}\widehat{\mu}_i(a).\label{eq:predictions}\end{equation}} The resulting curves compare expected support across appearance values while keeping the other observed characteristics in each gender group constant. Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes} presents these differences as appearance-associated electoral inequality within gender.\footnote{Online Appendix \autoref{eq:share-predictions} defines average municipal vote shares, and Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-share} expresses the same fitted model on that scale.} These predictions describe the allocation of personal votes. They do not estimate the probability of winning a council seat.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/predicted_votes.pdf}
\caption{Predicted expected personal votes in the observational natural experiment, with baseline controls. Curves vary the atypical cue contrast over the within-gender \Sexpr{paper_values$prediction_percentiles[1]}th--\Sexpr{paper_values$prediction_percentiles[2]}th percentile range and average over observed candidate characteristics, parties, and municipalities. Shading gives municipality-clustered \Sexpr{paper_values$confidence_pct}\% confidence intervals for the conditional mean. Rug strips beneath each panel show observed candidates within the displayed cue range. Panel headings report the full gender-group sample sizes. Predictions describe conditional associations.}
\label{fig:predicted-votes}
\end{figure}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes} presents the observational natural experiment's main results as unequal expected personal support within gender categories.\footnote{Table~\ref{tab:oa-baseline-regression} in the Online Appendix presents the corresponding Poisson regression table, reporting coefficients and municipality-clustered standard errors for the baseline model underlying Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes}.} Table~\ref{tab:main} quantifies appearance-associated inequality within gender using the contrast in \autoref{eq:model}. A one-SD increase in the contrast is associated with differences in expected votes of \Sexpr{effect_text("Male")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text("Female")} for women. \Sexpr{primary_interval_text} \Sexpr{primary_evidence_text} To distinguish inequality within gender from differences in its appearance association across genders, we compare the two associations directly. The interaction test gives $p=\Sexpr{fmt(gender_diff_p,3)}$. \Sexpr{primary_gender_comparison_text}
\par

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption{Gender-atypical appearance and expected personal votes with baseline controls}
\label{tab:main}
<<main-table, results='asis'>>=
cat(main_contrast_table_tex)
@
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}\footnotesize
Entries are percentage differences in expected votes for a one-SD increase in the atypical cue contrast, with municipality-clustered \Sexpr{paper_values$confidence_pct}\% confidence intervals in brackets. The model includes \Sexpr{n_main} candidates in \Sexpr{n_cities} municipalities, with municipality and party fixed effects, age, and attractiveness.
\end{minipage}
\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes} shows how these estimates distribute expected personal support among candidates sharing a gender category. \Sexpr{primary_inequality_text} \Sexpr{primary_conformity_interpretation} The inequality among male candidacies therefore challenges an account in which conformity always concentrates support among more gender-typical candidates. Instead, expected votes can be higher for a profile that departs from masculine appearance expectations. This gives Monk's argument an electoral implication. Atypicality can mark advantage as well as disadvantage within a shared category \parencite{Monk2022}.

Explaining this distribution of expected votes requires asking why a less gender-typical male profile could be favored. The cooperative-leadership account supplies one possibility. More feminine liberal men were judged warmer in \textcite{CarpinellaJohnson2013b}, while \textcite{SpisakWarriors2012} experimentally showed that feminine-looking leadership gained value in peacekeeping rather than war scenarios. Those findings identify a possible route from cue balance to unequal support among men if some voters infer and value cooperation. They do not establish that Finnish voters made those inferences. \Sexpr{primary_cooperative_benchmark} The interaction test likewise leaves a distinct male--female association unestablished, so this inequality pattern does not identify a mechanism confined to male candidates.

This interpretation also explains why the result cannot be reduced to a separate masculinity premium. \textcite{Park2023} report a positive overall association between perceived masculinity and US vote share, whereas our contrast concerns the balance of masculinity and femininity relative to same-gender peers. The two results therefore concern different sources of appearance-associated inequality.\footnote{Our index can increase through higher femininity, lower masculinity, or both, so its association does not identify a separate return to either cue. \textcite{Park2023} evaluate whole campaign photographs and enter the cues separately.} A within-gender average can nevertheless combine contexts in which different appearance profiles carry different value. We therefore ask whether the appearance-associated inequality in Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes} extends across party settings.

\subsection{Party-specific associations}
The contextual expectation implies that inequality within a gender category may take a different direction or magnitude across political settings. We exploit the dataset's party information to examine this implication, allowing cue slopes and gender intercepts to vary by candidate party while retaining the baseline controls. Let $\log\mu_i^{\mathrm{baseline}}$ denote the linear predictor in \autoref{eq:model}. The secondary specification is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{party}} &= \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{baseline}}
 +\sum_{p\ne p_0}\mathbf{1}(\texttt{Party}_i=p)
 \left[\delta_p\texttt{Woman}_i+\kappa_p\texttt{Atypicality}_i\right.
 \\[-0.1em]
 &\qquad\left.+\tau_p(\texttt{Woman}_i\times\texttt{Atypicality}_i)\right],
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:party-model}
\end{equation}
Relative to reference party $p_0$, the additional terms allow the atypicality association to vary across parties and by gender within each party. All coefficients are re-estimated in the expanded Poisson specification, which retains the baseline log link and expresses these associations as proportional differences in expected votes. Party disaggregation exploits the same observational natural experiment and does not introduce a separate source of assignment. Estimation still uses candidate-level variation conditional on the observed controls and fixed effects, with the same limits on causal identification as the baseline specification. Candidate party identifies the political setting of the comparison, but it does not identify the ideology of the voters who cast the ballots. The atypicality scale remains the within-gender scale defined in \autoref{eq:axes}. The model includes \Sexpr{party_model_n} candidates in \Sexpr{length(eligible_party_levels)} parties and \Sexpr{party_model_clusters} municipalities.\footnote{We restrict party-specific comparisons to parties with at least \Sexpr{paper_values$support_min_candidates} candidates in each gender category. This excludes \Sexpr{paper_values$party_exclusion_text}, totaling \Sexpr{paper_values$party_model_dropped_n} candidates. They remain in the primary analysis. Online Appendix Table~\ref{tab:oa-party} reports the party-specific contrasts and candidate and municipality counts.}

Figure~\ref{fig:party-votes} disaggregates the appearance-associated inequality summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes}.\footnote{Figure~\ref{fig:party-votes} expresses these associations as expected personal votes, averaging over the observed candidate characteristics and municipalities within each party and gender. Rug strips show the distribution of observed cue profiles. Confidence bands are omitted to focus on substantive point-estimate patterns. Full municipality-clustered intervals for the one-SD contrasts and joint heterogeneity tests are reported in \autoref{sec:oa-party} of the Online Appendix. Differences in panel levels also reflect the composition of these groups, and the panels use separate vertical scales. Proportional contrasts in Table~\ref{tab:oa-party} therefore provide the direct comparison of slopes. Cells with few candidates or municipalities offer limited information about their party-specific association. Their separate levels do not estimate a gender gap in support.} \Sexpr{party_profile_interpretation}

\Sexpr{party_female_context_interpretation} This is the inequality implication of the contextual expectation. The same relative cue shift can be associated with advantage in one party context and disadvantage in another, so gender membership alone cannot describe the appearance-linked distribution of electoral support.

Because these contrasts allow the value of a cue to differ within gender and across parties, they speak to the candidate gender-by-party differences reported by \textcite{Carpinella2016}. In their actual-election models, less masculine Republican men and more feminine Republican women were more likely to win. Their results therefore also separate a feminine-cue advantage from a uniform atypicality penalty. Their vote-margin analysis did not reproduce that binary-election pattern, reinforcing the distinction between gaining support and winning office. Our personal-vote counts and gender-relative contrast extend the question to support allocated within Finnish party lists, rather than reproducing their US party pattern or binary election outcome.

The difference between receiving votes and winning office does not remove the question of why cue value changes across political settings. The varying associations fit the contextual argument in \textcite{LaustsenPetersen2016}, but their dominance measure and ideological audiences differ from our appearance contrast and candidate-party groups. A possible leadership-fit explanation consequently remains a claim about why the cue's value might vary, rather than an identified explanation of these party associations.

\Sexpr{party_precision_text} The panels thus inform the contextual account of inequality, while uncertainty limits claims about individual party advantages. That qualification also applies to the distribution of expected support among men, whose apparent advantages depend on covariate and sample choices.\footnote{Online Appendix Table~\ref{tab:oa-party-sensitivity} reports these comparisons. The National Coalition male contrast changes from \Sexpr{party_point_text("KOK", "Male")} to \Sexpr{party_point_text("KOK", "Male", "party_experience")} with prior-office controls. The Green male contrast changes from \Sexpr{party_point_text("VIHR", "Male")} to \Sexpr{party_point_text("VIHR", "Male", "party_trim")} after excluding the highest-vote \Sexpr{paper_values$trim_percent[1]}\% of candidates. \Sexpr{party_sdp_magnitude_note}} The party breakdown therefore qualifies the inequality in Figure~\ref{fig:predicted-votes}. Its within-male association varies across parties, and the uncertain pooled female association does not establish an absence of appearance-associated inequality within female candidacies.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/party_predicted_votes.pdf}
\caption{Predicted expected personal votes by party and gender, with baseline controls. Curves vary the cue contrast over each party--gender group's \Sexpr{paper_values$prediction_percentiles[1]}th--\Sexpr{paper_values$prediction_percentiles[2]}th percentile range and average over that group's observed candidate contexts. Each solid curve shows the continuous fitted predictions throughout this range. \Sexpr{party_band_note} Online Appendix Table~\ref{tab:oa-party} reports intervals for the corresponding one-SD contrasts. Rug strips show observed candidates within the displayed cue range, with men (M) above women (W). Panel headings report full group sample sizes. Vertical scales differ between panels.}
\label{fig:party-votes}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Distinguishing gendered appearance from attractiveness}
\label{sec:attractiveness}
Does the appearance-associated inequality simply reflect differences in attractiveness? This is a consequential alternative because attractive candidates can receive more votes \parencite{BerggrenEtAl2010,Bahamonde2024}. If the gender-atypical index merely captured attractiveness, it would add little to the explanation of unequal support. Our original dataset allows us to examine that possibility directly because attractiveness, masculinity, and femininity were rated separately. We exploit these measurements to compare how the scores relate to one another and whether accounting for attractiveness explains the appearance-associated differences in votes.

Figure~\ref{fig:attractiveness-pairs} pairs each candidate's gender cue balance with that candidate's attractiveness rating, separately for men and women. This comparison shows whether relatively feminine appearance is associated with greater measured attractiveness among candidates of the same gender. We use the signed cue balance $B=A$ among men and $B=-A$ among women. This orientation places relatively masculine profiles at lower values and relatively feminine profiles at higher values in both groups, with zero corresponding to each group's average balance.

Among men, the two measurements have little linear or rank-order relationship. The Pearson correlation is \Sexpr{attractiveness_correlation_text("Male")}, and the Spearman correlation is \Sexpr{attractiveness_correlation_text("Male","spearman")}. A more feminine appearance relative to masculine appearance therefore does not simply pick out the men rated as more attractive. Among women, the corresponding correlations are \Sexpr{attractiveness_correlation_text("Female")} and \Sexpr{attractiveness_correlation_text("Female","spearman")}. Women rated as more feminine relative to masculine are also commonly rated as more attractive. The data therefore distinguish these appearance dimensions more clearly among men than among women.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/attractiveness_cue_pairs.pdf}
\caption{Gender cue balance and attractiveness by recorded gender. Each point pairs one candidate's signed cue balance $B$ with that candidate's mean attractiveness rating. Lines summarize the paired relationships using descriptive least-squares fits. $B$ increases with femininity relative to masculinity within either gender. $B=A$ for men and $B=-A$ for women. Headings report group sample sizes. Attractiveness uses the \Sexpr{paper_values$scale_min}--\Sexpr{paper_values$scale_max} rating scale.}
\label{fig:attractiveness-pairs}
\end{figure}

The next comparison asks whether attractiveness adjustment accounts for the observed difference in electoral support. Table~\ref{tab:attractiveness-adjustment} compares three Poisson specifications on the same \Sexpr{n_main} candidates. The first omits attractiveness, the baseline includes it as a linear control, and the third allows the attractiveness association to vary by gender and take a nonlinear form. The flexible specification uses natural cubic splines with \Sexpr{paper_values$attractiveness_spline_df} degrees of freedom, interacted with gender. All three retain the same age, party, and municipality controls and the same gender-atypical index. This comparison changes the treatment of measured attractiveness while holding the other parts of the comparison constant.

