\Sexpr{attractiveness_male_adjustment_text} \Sexpr{attractiveness_adjustment_interpretation}

\Sexpr{attractiveness_female_adjustment_text}

These findings give attractiveness different implications for the interpretation of inequality among men and among women. Among men, the cue balance has little relationship with measured attractiveness, and the appearance-associated advantage remains after the flexible adjustment. The evidence therefore weighs against explaining this inequality solely through the attractiveness scores available in the dataset. Among women, the close relationship between the ratings makes their separate contributions harder to distinguish. This comparison strengthens the interpretation of a distinct conditional appearance association among men while preserving the uncertainty surrounding support among women. Causal identification of the underlying process remains a further task. Conditioning on attractiveness does not identify a direct effect of gendered appearance or rule out a pathway through attractiveness. The adjusted associations also leave open whether unmeasured aspects of attractiveness or shared perceptual judgments contribute to the votes candidates receive.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
Does departure from gender stereotypes carry an electoral penalty? We exploit an observational natural experiment through an original dataset of separately rated appearance cues and linked electoral returns to frame the answer as a question about inequality within gender. In the baseline comparison, expected support is distributed unequally among male candidacies, favoring a cue profile that departs from masculine appearance expectations. The corresponding female association remains uncertain. That uncertainty does not establish an equal distribution of support among women. Party disaggregation further shows that a gender-level average can conceal different appearance associations within political contexts. Thus, the conditional patterns do not show a uniform conformity penalty, while locating the question of advantage within categories and settings.

This location of inequality develops the infracategorical perspective \parencite{Monk2022}. Treating men as a comparatively advantaged category does not determine which male appearance profiles receive the most support. Similarly, uncertainty about a pooled female association does not exhaust the distinctions among female candidates. The relevant question is how candidates are positioned within their gender category and how that position acquires value in a political context. Warmth and cooperative leadership provide one possible explanation of the distribution of expected votes among men \parencite{CarpinellaJohnson2013b,SpisakWarriors2012}. Because the party profiles vary, however, any such explanation must account for differences in what audiences infer and value. It cannot assume a common meaning for every departure.

Personal votes determine candidates' positions within electoral lists \parencite{HolliWass2010,SoderlundVonSchoultz2024}. Unequal support associated with appearance can therefore matter for representation, although our model does not estimate seat-winning probabilities. Because these differences arise within gender and party contexts, comparing men and women as groups can overlook inequalities among candidates of the same gender.

Our natural-experiment claim concerns an external probabilistic component in appearance, while our empirical findings concern the unequal distribution of support conditional on observed attributes. Making this distinction explicit allows naturally arising variation to inform an infracategorical account without treating the natural-experiment label as a substitute for causal identification. The Finnish recruitment and campaign setting provides contextual grounds for the assignment premise \parencite[628--629]{Bahamonde2024}. The interpretation nevertheless depends on whether the assumed external variation remains present in the photographed cues and among the candidates represented by the dataset.

Several limitations qualify the natural-experiment classification itself. Neither a genetic contribution to facial morphology nor candidates' incomplete control of their appearance demonstrates a nondegenerate assignment distribution for our perceived cue contrast. The external probabilistic component is an explicit substantive assumption whose unit-level probabilities cannot be recovered from these data \parencite[118,125]{Titiunik2021}. Grooming, facial expression, camera angle, and portrait selection may affect the same ratings. These choices are compatible with the framework if an uncontrollable component remains, but a measure dominated by strategic presentation would weaken the classification. Broad recruitment of residents reduces concern about an exclusively professional candidate pool. It does not rule out selective entry, nomination, or photograph availability. Conditioning on candidacy can also induce dependence between appearance and vote-relevant resources, even if some earlier appearance variation arose by chance. The appearance-assignment premise must therefore apply within the selected candidate population, rather than being inferred from biological variation in the population at large.

Further limitations concern causal identification within this observational subtype. External assignment need not imply exchangeability, and inherited or developmental determinants of appearance may also be related to family background, health, or other determinants of electoral support \parencite[119,122--124]{Titiunik2021}. Recognition and campaign resources can likewise remain associated with both cues and votes. The index can increase through different combinations of femininity and masculinity, so equal index values need not represent a single well-defined intervention. Attractiveness, party affiliation, and prior political office are also not automatically pre-assignment covariates when the proposed external component originates before a candidate's political career. Their adjustment does not identify a direct appearance effect. Finally, candidates compete for votes within shared elections, leaving potential interference across candidates relevant to any future causal estimand. For these reasons, the fixed effects, controls, and natural-experiment classification do not establish conditional unconfoundedness, treatment-version consistency, or an identified causal response to appearance.

The scope and precision of the observed inequality require additional qualifications. Estimates depend on prior-office adjustment and unusually high-vote candidates.\footnote{Online Appendix Table~\ref{tab:oa-sensitivity} compares municipality-by-party fixed effects, separate-gender specifications, and upper-tail vote exclusions. Figures~\ref{fig:oa-votes} and~\ref{fig:oa-share} compare predictions with and without incumbency and parliamentary-office controls. Table~\ref{tab:oa-party-sensitivity} reports the corresponding party comparisons.} The post-election ratings reflect Finnish-speaking adults in the survey's age range, rather than the impressions or ideology of actual voters. The vote-model intervals omit uncertainty in candidates' mean ratings. The displayed cue ranges document observed support, but they do not establish counterfactual positivity for every candidate \parencite[125--126]{Titiunik2021}. Neither recorded gender nor the index measures gender identity or categorization uncertainty. These limits concern the cause, measurement, and scope of inequality. They do not establish that support is distributed equally.

Future research can strengthen the assignment argument by comparing repeated portraits and facial-structure measures to determine how much of the rated cue variation persists across presentation choices. Information on prospective candidates and party recruitment would also clarify whether selection changes the distribution of naturally arising profiles. Such evidence would address the external assignment premise without, by itself, establishing exchangeability. Experiments can then strengthen causal identification by varying gendered appearance and attractiveness separately in matched portraits. Cooperative and competitive leadership tasks would test when voters favor each appearance. Warmth, competence, and dominance ratings would identify inferred qualities. Gender-categorization confidence and response time would distinguish these judgments from uncertainty about whether a person appears male or female. Measuring voter ideology and varying party labels would separate audience preferences from party context. Replication across elections, including older and Swedish-speaking raters, would test how widely the findings apply.

