\section{Natural assignment and the scope of identification}
\label{sec:oa-natural-experiment}

We formalize the natural-experiment claim as an observational assignment argument \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. This classification rests on an explicit assumption about the source of variation in the campaign portraits. We assume that inherited and developmental processes contribute a stochastic component beyond candidates' precise control, while recruitment and presentation choices can contribute systematic components. Broad recruitment of ordinary residents and the prominent use of headshots in posters and newspaper advertisements support this interpretation in Finnish municipal elections \parencite[628--629]{Bahamonde2024}. The classification neither establishes exchangeability nor identifies a causal effect on personal votes.

\subsection{The appearance contrast and its assignment mechanism}

Let $C_i$ indicate candidacy and $I_i$ indicate inclusion in the estimation sample, with $I_i=1$ implying $C_i=1$. The selected indices in recorded gender category $g$ are $\mathcal I_g=\{i\mid I_i=1,g_i=g\}$. All assignment statements below concern this selected candidate population. Let $E_i$ summarize naturally arising appearance components, $L_i$ presentation choices, and $H_i$ other features of portrait production. The portrait $P_i$ gives rise to population-level femininity and masculinity impressions $F_i^\ast$ and $M_i^\ast$. Their gender-relative contrast $A_i^\ast$ is the appearance quantity approximated by the observed rating-based index $A_i$. A conceptual representation is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 P_i&=h_g(E_i,L_i,H_i),\\
 (F_i^\ast,M_i^\ast)&=r_g(P_i),\\
 A_i^\ast&=\phi_g(F_i^\ast,M_i^\ast;\vartheta_g).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:ne-generation}
\end{equation}

The mapping $\phi_g$ preserves the orientation of \autoref{eq:axes}. For a fixed reference distribution, collect its centering and scaling constants in $\vartheta_g=(\mu_g^F,\sigma_g^F,\mu_g^M,\sigma_g^M,\sigma_g^\Delta)$. The final constant $\sigma_g^\Delta$ is the reference standard deviation of the difference between the two standardized ratings. With $s_g=+1$ for men and $s_g=-1$ for women, the mapping is
\begin{equation}
 \phi_g(f,m;\vartheta_g)
 =\frac{s_g}{\sigma_g^\Delta}
 \left(\frac{f-\mu_g^F}{\sigma_g^F}
       -\frac{m-\mu_g^M}{\sigma_g^M}\right).
\label{eq:ne-contrast}
\end{equation}

The empirical index uses the sample counterparts of these constants. The conceptual assignment discussion holds the reference distribution fixed, so that a different appearance realization does not redefine every candidate's scale. The distinction between $A_i^\ast$ and $A_i$ formalizes the difference between appearance and its survey measurement. It does not introduce a fitted latent-variable model or change the index used in estimation.

Let $\mathbf X_g^0$ collect characteristics determined before the relevant appearance-assignment process. This set need not coincide with the regression controls. Let $\mathcal V_g=\{\mathbf V_g(\mathbf p)\mid\mathbf p\in\mathcal P\}$ denote potential personal-vote profiles under fully specified campaign-presentation assignments. The complete portrait-assignment vector $\mathbf p$ covers the relevant competitors as well as the focal candidates. Indexing these profiles by $\mathbf p$ avoids assuming either that every portrait yielding the same scalar contrast has the same consequences or that competitors' presentations cannot affect a candidate's votes. The potential-outcome notation distinguishes assignment from exchangeability and is not an identified causal parameter. Write the joint appearance-assignment law as
\begin{equation}
 Q_g\!\left(d\mathbf a\mid
 \mathbf X_g^0,\mathcal V_g,\mathbf I_g=\mathbf 1\right).
\label{eq:ne-assignment-law}
\end{equation}

Condition $\widetilde D$ concerns the generation of $A_i^\ast$. Researchers did not design or implement the allocation of candidates' underlying appearance features. Designing the contrast and its standardization is a measurement operation rather than an allocation of those features.

Condition $\widetilde K$ concerns knowledge of the full law in \autoref{eq:ne-assignment-law}. The algebra defining $\phi_g$ is known, but the probabilities generating alternative appearance allocations are unknown and cannot be reconstructed from the available candidate records. Unknown and unknowable allocation probabilities distinguish this assignment mechanism from an external lottery with a recoverable allocation protocol \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. The requirement concerns the full assignment law for this design, rather than a claim that every underlying biological process is beyond scientific investigation. Known coding rules and an estimated distribution of realized scores do not reveal the joint allocation probabilities conditional on potential vote profiles.

Condition $\widetilde P$ is the substantive probabilistic-assignment assumption. We assume that variation in $E_i$ induces a nondegenerate assignment distribution for $A_i^\ast$ through the mappings in \autoref{eq:ne-generation}, even though candidates can influence $L_i$ and $H_i$. This assumption must concern the cue contrast itself. Variation confined to other facial features or to the joint level of the ratings would not establish it. The externality is relative to candidates' precise control, allowing systematic presentation choices to coexist with an external chance component \parencite[118--119]{Titiunik2021}. The Finnish recruitment and campaign practices support the assumption that naturally arising variation remains visible in the portraits. They do not establish the magnitude of the biological contribution, equal assignment probabilities, or independence from potential electoral support.

\subsection{Probabilistic assignment for a continuous contrast}

The binary requirement that each unit have a positive probability of either treatment condition has a local support analogue for $A_i^\ast$. Let $Q_{ig}$ be the marginal assignment law for candidate $i$. For two distinct cue values $a_0$ and $a_1$, choose disjoint neighborhoods $J_0$ and $J_1$ within the range relevant to the proposed comparison. The probabilistic assumption for this comparison is
\begin{equation}
 0<Q_{ig}\!\left(J_k\mid
 \mathbf X_g^0,\mathcal V_g,\mathbf I_g=\mathbf 1\right)<1,
 \qquad k\in\{0,1\},\quad i\in\mathcal I_g.
\label{eq:ne-probabilistic-support}
\end{equation}

The probability-kernel formulation does not require an absolutely continuous distribution. If a density exists, the neighborhood probabilities can be written as
\begin{equation}
 Q_{ig}\!\left(J_k\mid
 \mathbf X_g^0,\mathcal V_g,\mathbf I_g=\mathbf 1\right)
 =\int_{J_k}q_{ig}\!\left(a\mid
 \mathbf X_g^0,\mathcal V_g,\mathbf I_g=\mathbf 1\right)\,da.
\label{eq:ne-assignment-density}
\end{equation}

Positive density on the neighborhoods is a sufficient support condition, without implying a uniform density or common probabilities across candidates. Positive probability at each exact real-valued score is not required. The observed rating averages may be discretized, which further motivates expressing the condition through neighborhoods. Neither the sample range nor a rug plot establishes \autoref{eq:ne-probabilistic-support} for each candidate. The probabilistic condition rests on an assignment argument and is not verified by observed score variation alone \parencite[118,125--126]{Titiunik2021}.

\subsection{Survey randomization and appearance measurement}

Let $\mathcal R_i^F$ and $\mathcal R_i^M$ contain the raters assigned to evaluate the femininity and masculinity of candidate $i$. For their attribute-specific ratings $R_{ri}^F$ and $R_{ri}^M$, the observed quantities are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 F_i&=\frac{1}{|\mathcal R_i^F|}
       \sum_{r\in\mathcal R_i^F}R_{ri}^F,\\
 M_i&=\frac{1}{|\mathcal R_i^M|}
       \sum_{r\in\mathcal R_i^M}R_{ri}^M,\\
 A_i&=\phi_g(F_i,M_i;\widehat\vartheta_g).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:ne-rating-measurement}
\end{equation}

Random photograph assignment operates on the rater--portrait pairing, according to researcher-designed survey rules. It does not assign $E_i$, $L_i$, or $P_i$ among candidates and does not assign portraits to actual voters. The rating survey followed the election, so this randomization cannot identify an effect on votes already cast. It supports appearance measurement, while random photograph sampling concerns the composition of the available portrait sample. Neither procedure supplies the appearance-assignment law in \autoref{eq:ne-assignment-law}. The vote-model intervals condition on the realized candidate averages rather than propagating their measurement uncertainty.

\subsection{Exchangeability and candidate selection}

The natural-experiment classification does not impose conditional exchangeability. For an unconfoundedness-based interpretation under a specified presentation intervention and target population, an additional necessary restriction would have the form
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal V_g\ \perp\!\!\!\perp\ \mathbf A_g^\ast
 \mid\mathbf X_g^0,\mathbf I_g=\mathbf 1.
\label{eq:ne-exchangeability}
\end{equation}

We do not assume \autoref{eq:ne-exchangeability}. The restriction would not by itself define a causal contrast for the scalar index $A$. Different portrait versions can produce the same index value, and interference among competing candidates can affect the allocation of votes. A causal interpretation would additionally require a specified presentation intervention and assumptions about how other candidates' presentations enter the potential vote profiles. Neither requirement is imposed here. An external stochastic component can generate probabilistic assignment while the probabilities remain related to potential outcomes. Nor do candidate-level covariate adjustments recover an unknown assignment law merely by being included in the regression. Information about the assignment process and genuinely predetermined covariates would be required to support a conditional comparability argument \parencite[122--126]{Titiunik2021}.

Selection into candidacy is especially relevant when the external component precedes recruitment. Let $U_i$ summarize other determinants of nomination and electoral support, and consider a density for $E_i$ conditional on genuinely prior characteristics $X_i^0=x$. Bayes' rule gives
\begin{equation}
 f(e\mid C_i=1,x,u)
 =\frac{\Pr(C_i=1\mid E_i=e,x,u)f(e\mid x,u)}
 {\displaystyle\int
   \Pr(C_i=1\mid E_i=t,x,u)f(t\mid x,u)\,dt}.
\label{eq:ne-candidacy-selection}
\end{equation}

Even if $f(e\mid x,u)=f(e\mid x)$ before nomination, the selected distribution can depend on $u$ through the nomination probability. Broad resident recruitment supports the argument that naturally arising appearance variation enters competition, but does not establish independence after conditioning on $C_i=1$. Photograph availability, usability, recognition exclusions, and complete observations impose further selection through $I_i$. The probabilistic support condition must therefore concern the selected population actually analyzed. The study does not recover an always-candidate population whose membership would remain fixed under alternative lifelong appearance realizations.

\subsection{Assignment time and the descriptive estimand}

The inherited component begins before candidacy, while presentation choices and developmental influences can occur at later points. Let $t_0$ denote the origin of that inherited component, $t_C$ candidacy, $t_E$ the election, and $t_R$ survey measurement. Let $t_P$ denote the availability of the campaign portrait to voters. The relevant temporal relations are
\begin{equation}
 t_0<t_C<t_E<t_R,
 \qquad t_P<t_E.
\label{eq:ne-chronology}
\end{equation}

This ordering does not imply that the entire adult cue profile was fixed at $t_0$, nor that its full assignment process has an observed time stamp. A hypothetical lifelong appearance intervention and an intervention on a campaign portrait would have different time origins and interpretations. Political resources, party affiliation, office holding, and attractiveness may lie downstream of earlier appearance components. They cannot automatically be treated as pretreatment covariates for a lifelong appearance contrast. Falsification and adjustment therefore require distinguishing genuinely prior characteristics from variables on later pathways \parencite[122--126]{Titiunik2021}.

The fitted model retains the descriptive conditional mean in \autoref{eq:model}. Writing the sample restrictions explicitly, its target is
\begin{equation}
 m_g(a,\mathbf x,c,p)
 =\mathrm E\!\left[
 \texttt{Votes}_i\mid A_i=a,g_i=g,\mathbf X_i=\mathbf x,
 c(i)=c,p(i)=p,I_i=1\right].
\label{eq:ne-observational-mean}
\end{equation}

Variation conditional on the observed controls identifies the regression's appearance association under its conditional-mean specification. It does not convert \autoref{eq:ne-observational-mean} into an interventional expectation or separate the biological component from presentation choices. The natural-experiment classification concerns the proposed source of probabilistic appearance variation. The figures and contrasts continue to describe unequal expected personal support within the selected candidate population, without estimating causal effects of changing appearance.

\section{Balance between raw ratings}
\label{sec:oa-balance}
An alternative specification assesses whether balance between the raw femininity and masculinity ratings is associated with electoral support. It replaces the directional contrast of within-gender standardized ratings with raw cue balance and raw cue intensity, defined in \autoref{eq:raw-balance}.
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \texttt{RawBalance}_i &= 1-\frac{|F_i-M_i|}{\Sexpr{paper_values$scale_max-paper_values$scale_min}},\\
 \texttt{RawIntensity}_i &= (F_i+M_i)/2.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:raw-balance}
\end{equation}
We standardize each index within gender. The balance associations are \Sexpr{effect_text_balance("Male")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text_balance("Female")} for women. This alternative concerns equality of raw ratings, which is distinct from equality relative to gender-specific means and does not establish categorical uncertainty.

\section{Joint cue level as an additional adjustment}
The directional contrast summarizes relative cue balance but omits the combined rating level. Candidates can have the same directional difference while their combined femininity and masculinity rating levels vary. To assess whether this additional variation changes the atypicality association, we define joint cue level as
\begin{equation}
 S_i=\frac{z^F_i+z^M_i}{\operatorname{sd}_g(z^F+z^M)}.
 \label{eq:joint-level}
\end{equation}
As a standardized sum, $S$ can be elevated when either rating exceeds its gender-specific mean, even if the other falls below it. Conditional on a fixed atypicality contrast, an increase in $S$ corresponds to equal increases in both standardized ratings. The adjusted specification includes $S$ and its gender interaction.
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{joint}}&=\log\mu_i^{\mathrm{baseline}}
 +\rho_1\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i\\
 &\quad+\rho_2(\texttt{Woman}_i\times\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:joint-model}
\end{equation}
All baseline coefficients are re-estimated. This adjustment relaxes the equal-magnitude, opposite associations assigned to standardized femininity and masculinity by the primary contrast specification. The adjusted atypicality associations are \Sexpr{effect_text("Male", "joint")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text("Female", "joint")} for women. The joint-level associations are \Sexpr{s_effect_text("Male")} and \Sexpr{s_effect_text("Female")}, respectively. Figures~\ref{fig:oa-joint-votes} and~\ref{fig:oa-joint-share} compare the predictions from both specifications. Table~\ref{tab:oa-sensitivity} reports the corresponding atypicality contrasts. \Sexpr{oa_joint_inequality_text}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/oa_joint_predicted_votes.pdf}
\caption{Predicted expected personal votes with and without joint cue level. The adjusted curve holds $S=0$. The baseline model does not include $S$. Both specifications average predictions over the same gender-specific observed candidate contexts. Shading denotes municipality-clustered \Sexpr{paper_values$confidence_pct}\% confidence intervals.}
\label{fig:oa-joint-votes}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/oa_joint_predicted_vote_probability.pdf}
\caption{Average predicted municipal vote share with and without joint cue level. Predictions and confidence intervals follow Figure~\ref{fig:oa-joint-votes}. Denominators include all valid personal votes in each municipality.}
\label{fig:oa-joint-share}
\end{figure}

\FloatBarrier
\section{Quadratic cue configurations}
The joint-level adjustment permits the directional contrast and combined rating level to vary independently. Its linear predictor nevertheless estimates constant slopes of log expected votes for both cue indices within gender. To allow curvature and an atypicality slope that varies with joint level, the quadratic specification adds squared indices, their product, and gender interactions to \autoref{eq:joint-model}.
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{quadratic}} &= \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{joint}}
 +\eta_1\texttt{Atypicality}_i^2+\eta_2\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i^2
 \\
 &\quad+\eta_3\texttt{Atypicality}_i\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i \\
 &\quad+\texttt{Woman}_i\left(
 \eta_4\texttt{Atypicality}_i^2+\eta_5\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i^2
 \right)\\
 &\quad+\eta_6\texttt{Woman}_i\texttt{Atypicality}_i\texttt{JointCueLevel}_i.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:quadratic}
\end{equation}
All coefficients of the joint-level-adjusted linear predictor are re-estimated, so the added terms describe curvature and interaction conditional on the same controls. Table~\ref{tab:oa-quadratic} reports these nonlinear coefficients by gender. A joint Wald test evaluates whether the \Sexpr{nonlinear_df} added terms equal zero. It gives $F=\Sexpr{fmt(nonlinear_wald/nonlinear_df,2)}$ with \Sexpr{nonlinear_df} and \Sexpr{attr(vcovs$surface,"df")} degrees of freedom ($p=\Sexpr{fmt(nonlinear_p,3)}$). Table~\ref{tab:oa-nonlinear-tests} applies the same test after upper-tail exclusions, connecting the assessment of nonlinearity to dependence on high-vote candidates. Figure~\ref{fig:oa-surface} shows the fitted cue surface only where nearby candidate profiles provide empirical support. Figure~\ref{fig:cue-support} shows the underlying raw ratings. \Sexpr{oa_quadratic_inequality_text}

