\FloatBarrier
\section{Political-experience adjustment}
Political experience provides a further reason candidates may attract different numbers of votes even conditional on age, appearance ratings, party, and municipality. We therefore add indicators for incumbent municipal councillors and members of the national parliament, referring to office held before the election. Holding the sample and baseline controls fixed, the expanded specification adds
\begin{equation}
 \log\mu_i^{\mathrm{office}}=\log\mu_i^{\mathrm{baseline}}
 +\rho_1\texttt{IncumbentCouncillor}_i+\rho_2\texttt{NationalMP}_i.
 \label{eq:office}
\end{equation}
The baseline linear predictor $\log\mu_i^{\mathrm{baseline}}$ follows \autoref{eq:model}, with all coefficients re-estimated in the expanded specification. The resulting one-SD atypicality associations are \Sexpr{effect_text("Male", "experience")} for men and \Sexpr{effect_text("Female", "experience")} for women. Figures~\ref{fig:oa-votes} and~\ref{fig:oa-share} express these associations as expected votes and municipal vote shares alongside the baseline predictions. Both specifications average over the same observed candidate contexts. \Sexpr{oa_office_inequality_text}

